meta {
  name: Collect Order Item
  type: http
  seq: 8
}

patch {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/orders/{{orderId}}/items/{{orderItemId}}/collect?print=false
  body: none
  auth: bearer
}

params:query {
  ~print: false
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{businessAccessToken}}
}

tests {
  test("200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });
  test("item is collected", function () {
    const item = res.getBody().data.items.find((i) => i.id === bru.getEnvVar("orderItemId"));
    expect(item.collectedAt).to.be.a("string");
  });
}

docs {
  Mark one order item's sample as collected. Sets `collectedAt` (now) and
  `collectedBy` (the caller's person id) on the item and returns the refreshed,
  fully-composed order.

  Beyond the order-item flag, this drives the real accession sample lifecycle:
  the item's linked `AccessionSample`(s) still in a collectable status
  (NEW / HOLD / REPEAT) are transitioned to COLLECTED (an `AccessionStatusHistory`
  row is appended), and because a sample is one physical tube shared by several
  tests, every sibling order item on a transitioned sample is stamped collected
  too. Both writes share one tenant-scoped transaction.

  Query param `?print=true` ("Collect & Print") additionally assigns a barcode to
  the collected sample(s) that lack one (`BAR-#####-A`). Omit or `false` for a
  plain collect.

  Idempotent: a repeat call keeps the original `collectedAt`, and samples already
  past NEW/HOLD/REPEAT are skipped (no `INVALID_SAMPLE_TRANSITION`). Safe no-op
  when the order has no samples yet (e.g. a DRAFT / non-diagnostic order): only
  `collectedAt` is set. 404 `ORDER_NOT_FOUND` if the order is missing/other-tenant;
  404 `ORDER_ITEM_NOT_FOUND` if the item does not belong to that order.
}
